            <ArticleTitle>Perforation of the gastric remnant in a patient post-Roux-en-Y gastric bypass.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2015-213862</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2015213862</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>With the dramatic increase in obesity surgery and the subsequent increase in ageing post-gastric bypass patients, early recognition of possible and serious complications is of the utmost importance. We present a case of a 33-year-old woman who presented to the emergency room, with progressive epigastric pain. The patient had undergone laparoscopic Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery 14 months earlier. Diagnostic laparoscopy was performed and showed a prepyloric perforation of the gastric remnant. The defect was closed and omentoplasty was performed. The patient was put on lifelong proton pump inhibitors.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>2016 BMJ Publishing Group Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
